<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amsterdam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amsterdam is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and was the commercial and financial heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> in the seventeenth century. It hosted the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>'s largest chamber, the Amsterdam Exchange Bank and a major stock market. The city's wealth and institutions underpinned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amsterdam.jpg" alt="Amsterdam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrés Barrios · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imagen_de_los_canales_conc%C3%A9ntricos_en_%C3%81msterdam.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sixteenth-century Antwerp was the first great hub of a world economy linking the Baltic, England, southern Germany, the Mediterranean and the Iberian empires, and its commercial techniques passed to <b>Amsterdam</b> after 1585 along with many of its merchants and craftsmen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…intra-Asian trading network, which earned profits by carrying goods between Asian ports — Japanese silver and copper, Indian textiles, Indonesian spices — independently of European demand, was its most innovative feature, allowing the company to finance much of its Asian business without constant transfers of bullion from <b>Amsterdam</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Amsterdam</b> became the staple market of European trade and finance, with the Bank of Amsterdam (1609) and an exchange where company shares were bought and sold.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch merchants, with larger ships, cheaper freight and a stronger financial base in <b>Amsterdam</b>, took over much of the Baltic grain and timber trade passing through the Danish Sound, while the herring shoals moved from the Baltic to the North Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The country’s first railway, between <b>Amsterdam</b> and Haarlem, had opened in 1839, symbolising a slow turn towards modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>New Amsterdam, outside Fort <b>Amsterdam</b>, became a hub for trade between North America, the Caribbean and Europe, loading pelts, lumber and tobacco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
